\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.1}{\ignorespaces Structural diagnostic summary by perturbation mode. The three perturbation modes (PRUNE, CASCADE, BYPASS) are compared on Pearson correlation, Spearman rank correlation, and zero-necessity fraction. Error bars indicate 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals. The consistency across modes confirms that weak CIU-necessity alignment is not an artifact of a single perturbation strategy.}}{8}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mode-comparison}{{C.1}{8}{Structural diagnostic summary by perturbation mode. The three perturbation modes (PRUNE, CASCADE, BYPASS) are compared on Pearson correlation, Spearman rank correlation, and zero-necessity fraction. Error bars indicate 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals. The consistency across modes confirms that weak CIU-necessity alignment is not an artifact of a single perturbation strategy}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.2}{\ignorespaces Redundancy density and compensation analysis. Panel (a): per-trace redundancy density distribution (mean $0.3842$). Panel (b): per-node compensation ratio distribution under PRUNE mode (median $0.000$, mean $0.0084$). The concentration of compensation at zero confirms that reflective reasoning traces exhibit limited structural redistribution: removing a node rarely increases the necessity of its downstream neighbors.}}{8}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:redundancy-comp}{{C.2}{8}{Redundancy density and compensation analysis. Panel (a): per-trace redundancy density distribution (mean $0.3842$). Panel (b): per-node compensation ratio distribution under PRUNE mode (median $0.000$, mean $0.0084$). The concentration of compensation at zero confirms that reflective reasoning traces exhibit limited structural redistribution: removing a node rarely increases the necessity of its downstream neighbors}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.3}{\ignorespaces Resilience curves under ordered node removal. Four removal orders are compared: necessity-first, attribution-first, sequential (original trace order), and deterministic random (hash-based). The $y$-axis shows remaining structural necessity fraction; the $x$-axis is the fraction of nodes removed. AUC values are annotated. The necessity-first curve degrades sharply (AUC $0.1488$), while attribution-first (AUC $0.4761$) is close to random (AUC $0.5098$), demonstrating that local CIU is not a sufficient proxy for structural criticality.}}{9}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:resilience-app}{{C.3}{9}{Resilience curves under ordered node removal. Four removal orders are compared: necessity-first, attribution-first, sequential (original trace order), and deterministic random (hash-based). The $y$-axis shows remaining structural necessity fraction; the $x$-axis is the fraction of nodes removed. AUC values are annotated. The necessity-first curve degrades sharply (AUC $0.1488$), while attribution-first (AUC $0.4761$) is close to random (AUC $0.5098$), demonstrating that local CIU is not a sufficient proxy for structural criticality}{figure.3}{}}
